var e=`panchakosha-burnout-five-layers`,t=`Panchakosha for Burnout: Five Layers, Five Practices`,n=`October 2026`,r=`9 min read`,i=[`Burnout`,`Panchakosha`,`Taittirīya Upaniṣad`,`Practice`],a=[9,15],o=[`taittiriya-2-1`,`taittiriya-2-2-5`],s=`A contemplative analysis of Taittirīya Upaniṣad's Panchakosha (five sheaths) framework as an educational diagnostic for modern occupational burnout and somatic exhaustion.`,c=`Taittirīya Upaniṣad Brahmānanda Vallī, structured around Śaṅkara's commentary, adapted non-clinically as an educational self-reflection framework (Maslach 2001; Freudenberger 1974).`,l=`Educational and philosophical exploration only. Not a medical treatment for clinical depression, chronic fatigue syndrome, or occupational exhaustion.`,u=[{heading:`The Five Sheaths of Human Embodiment`,paragraphs:[`In Taittirīya Upaniṣad 2.1–5, the human organism is described not as a single solid block, but as five concentric layers or "sheaths" (koshas) nested within one another like russian matryoshka dolls:`,`1. Annamaya kosha: the physical sheath formed of food and nutrition.
2. Prāṇamaya kosha: the vital energy sheath of breath and autonomic circulation.
3. Manomaya kosha: the mental-emotional sheath of sensory perception, desires, and reactive feelings.
4. Vijñānamaya kosha: the wisdom sheath of intellect, discernment, and ethical orientation.
5. Ānandamaya kosha: the bliss sheath of deep experiential peace and wholeness.`,`Suffering occurs when identity gets "glued" to a single outer sheath. Burnout in modern life is almost always a collapse across multiple koshas misdiagnosed as merely "having too many emails."`]},{heading:`Mapping Burnout Across the Koshas`,paragraphs:[`Layer 1 (Annamaya): Chronic muscle tension, poor sleep, digestive distress, adrenal exhaustion. The practice: radical rest, nourishing diet, grounding contact with the earth.`,`Layer 2 (Prāṇamaya): Shallow chest breathing, sympathetic nervous system hyperarousal, depletion of vitality. The practice: conscious diaphragmatic pranayama, 4-in / 6-out breath pacing.`,`Layer 3 (Manomaya): Emotional irritability, cynicism, doom-scrolling, mental churn. The practice: sensory fasting, nature walks, conscious pause before reactive responses.`,`Layer 4 (Vijñānamaya): Loss of meaning, alienation from values, feelings of futility. The practice: philosophical self-inquiry (viveka), realigning work with enduring purpose.`,`Layer 5 (Ānandamaya): Disconnection from the silent ground of wholeness. The practice: meditation on the witness (sākṣī), resting in pure awareness.`]},{heading:`The Unshaken Center`,paragraphs:[`The crowning realization of the Taittirīya model is that the Ātman is not any of the five sheaths. The Self is that which observes all five sheaths.`,`When your body is fatigued, you can say: "My Annamaya sheath is tired, but the witness of fatigue is not tired." When your mind is anxious, you can say: "The emotional weather in the Manomaya sheath is stormy, but the sky in which the storm rages remains unhurt."`,`This dis-identification is the supreme technology of resilience: holding the wounded layer with compassion without confusing it with your ultimate reality.`]}],d=[`Taittirīya Upaniṣad with the Commentary of Śaṅkara, translated by Swami Gambhirananda, Advaita Ashrama, 1980.`,`Maslach, Christina, Schaufeli, Wilmar B., and Leiter, Michael P. "Job Burnout." Annual Review of Psychology, 52(1), 2001, pp. 397–422.`,`Freudenberger, Herbert J. "Staff Burn-Out." Journal of Social Issues, 30(1), 1974, pp. 159–165.`],f={slug:e,title:t,date:n,readingTime:r,tags:i,relatedChapters:a,relatedVerses:o,summary:s,viewOrigin:c,limits:l,sections:u,sources:d};export{n as date,f as default,l as limits,r as readingTime,a as relatedChapters,o as relatedVerses,u as sections,e as slug,d as sources,s as summary,i as tags,t as title,c as viewOrigin};